# Bash macros

Prefixes → [naming.mdc](naming.mdc). Bundle layout: [../../../scripts/bash_macros/README.md](../../../scripts/bash_macros/README.md).

Applies to `scripts/bash_macros/` (`load_macros` bundles) and to `scripts/bash_bringup/` / `scripts/bash_container/` (session bootstrap). Public macros (`build`, `cbuild`, `diag`, `load_macros`, `importer`) — **no** prefix.

Namespaces: `build::` / `diag::` / `load::` / `importer::` / `tr::` / … for macro helpers; `bringup::` and `container::` for bringup/container helpers.

Same helper names and completion must work on **Windows/Git Bash** (`./scripts/setup.bash macros`) and **native Linux** (macros or Distrobox). Do not add per-OS naming.

## Helpers are `ns::_name` bodies

Define the helper as `namespace::_name`. That string is the runtime name. Do not keep a short global `_name`, and do not wrap shorts with `_bind_namespace`.

```bash
# Bad — short global (collides across bundles)
_get_dir() { ... }
_bind_namespace tr _get_dir

# Bad — bundle name twice
tr::_tr_get_dir() { ... }

# Good — one name in source and at runtime
tr::_get_dir() { ... }
bringup::_is_sourced() { ... }
container::_init_ros2_env() { ... }
```

Call helpers as `tr::_get_dir` from public macros and from other helpers. The function name **must not** repeat the bundle name after `::`.

Bash has no private functions. `ns::_…` is a convention: unique across bundles, skipped by `load_macros` collision checks, hidden from TAB. The name stays in the shell.
